{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg9NtEzNQ8FdYoMPmUfxo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I don't have web access in this session, so the numbers below come from memory. Please check them against the linked sources before you rely on them. The figures depend heavily on which tokenizer and which text you measure.\n\n## OpenAI\n\n**OpenAI's own figures (GPT-4o launch post, May 2024)**\n\nOpenAI translated one sample sentence into 20 languages and reported token counts for the old and new tokenizers:\n\n| Tokenizer | English | Korean | Korean ÷ English |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `cl100k_base` (GPT-3.5 / GPT-4 / GPT-4 Turbo) | 27 | 45 | **≈1.67×** |\n| `o200k_base` (GPT-4o and later) | 24 | 27 | **≈1.13×** |\n\n- Source: OpenAI, \"Hello GPT-4o,\" May 13, 2024, https://openai.com/index/hello-gpt-4o/ (the \"Language tokenization\" section).\n- Caveat: this is a single short sentence, so treat it as illustrative rather than a benchmark.\n\n**Academic measurements on larger parallel corpora**\n\n- Petrov et al., \"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages,\" NeurIPS 2023, arXiv:2305.15425. They measure a \"tokenization premium\" relative to English on FLORES-200 for the GPT-2, `cl100k_base` and other tokenizers. Korean has a clear premium on `cl100k_base`, roughly 2× or more. I don't remember the exact figure, so take it from their tables.\n- Ahia et al., \"Do All Languages Cost the Same? Tokenization in the Era of Commercial Language Models,\" EMNLP 2023, arXiv:2305.13707. This paper looks at the same problem as API cost across languages on OpenAI models.\n\n## Claude (Anthropic)\n\n- As far as I know, Anthropic has not published per-language token ratios, and the Claude tokenizer is not open-source.\n- Anthropic does provide a token-counting API (`POST /v1/messages/count_tokens`), documented at https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/token-counting. That endpoint is the authoritative way to measure.\n- Anthropic's model docs also note that tokenizers change between model generations, so a ratio measured on one Claude model may not carry over to another.\n- Third-party blog measurements generally find that Claude uses more tokens than `o200k_base` on non-English text, Korean included. I can't give a reliable, citable Korean number from memory, so I won't quote one.\n\n## Bottom line\n\n- **OpenAI `cl100k_base`:** Korean takes about 1.7× (OpenAI's sample) to about 2×+ (FLORES-type corpora) the tokens of equivalent English.\n- **OpenAI `o200k_base`:** the gap shrinks to about 1.1–1.3×.\n- **Claude:** no official figure exists, so you'd need to measure it yourself.\n\nIf you'd like, I can write a short script that uses `tiktoken` for both OpenAI tokenizers and Anthropic's `count_tokens` for Claude, run on a parallel English/Korean set such as FLORES-200. That would give you Korean÷English ratios for each tokenizer on your own data. Note that it would send the sample text to Anthropic's API, so you'd need an API key."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2456,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2456},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"dba4c9cf-6bf0-4a9a-88f5-17611f0f3162","uuid":"f3c8272e-52ff-4745-8b7f-4fe96d440169","timestamp":"2026-10-18T08:21:57.348Z","request_id":"req_011Cg9NtEnDf6mAQ29DhHLnV"}
